-test, and we credit the separability-versus-clusterability phenomenon to prior work.
+The representation fed to a clustering algorithm may matter more than the algorithm itself in unsupervised analysis of the electrocardiogram (ECG), and this study measures how much it matters. Several clinically interesting questions are unsupervised: which patients group together, what subtypes exist, and whether structure can be found without labels that are expensive or unavailable. A natural first attempt is to cluster ECG records directly. This study began as a re-examination of a master's project that clustered whole 12-lead records in this way. The project flattened each strip into a long vector, compressed it with a dense autoencoder, and clustered the code. It reported high cluster purity and a silhouette score of 0.89 [GAP: source of the master's project results]. The purity equalled the majority-class proportion in every setting. The silhouette was measured on a degenerate solution in which almost all points fell into a single cluster. The conclusion of that work, that the difficulty lies in processing the raw signal rather than in the clustering step, is the starting point that this study tests.
+
+A representation can carry information about a label and still fail to cluster by it. Linear separability and clusterability are distinct properties: in self-supervised image models, representations with nearly the same linear-probe accuracy can differ in clustering quality \cite{sslclustering2025}. We read such a gap as a representation placing the discriminative direction away from its dominant axes of variation. A linear classifier then recovers the label while an unsupervised partition does not. This reading is ours, and we treat the distinction itself as background rather than as a finding. What has not been measured, for ECG, is how the common representation families compare in separability and clusterability under chance-corrected metrics.
+
+The closest benchmark, by Filice et al.~\cite{lookingbeyond2026}, compares ECG foundation models only with one another, without hand-crafted features and without the MIT-BIH database, and this study adds both. Filice et al. evaluated the models on four 12-lead datasets, PTB-XL among them. Beside downstream performance, their benchmark includes a representation-wise evaluation, which examines the structure of the embeddings themselves. Part of it is the adjusted Rand index (ARI) of Gaussian-mixture clusters of the frozen embeddings. The ARI measures the agreement between clusters and labels, corrected for chance. The authors stated that earlier ECG benchmarks lack a representation-wise evaluation. Hand-crafted features measure named physiological quantities, such as RR intervals and P-wave presence, and need no trained network. The MIT-BIH Arrhythmia Database was the first generally available set of standard test material for evaluating arrhythmia detectors \cite{mitbih2001}, and its heartbeats have been classified from feature sets of morphology and RR intervals \cite{dechazal2004}.
+
+We therefore compare five representations for the unsupervised separation of sinus rhythm (SR) from atrial fibrillation or flutter (AF), with the controls the original project lacked. Because atrial fibrillation differs from SR by definition in the regularity of the rhythm, we expected representations that encode rhythm to recover the split. Recent ECG foundation-model benchmarks compare models with one another or with supervised and random baselines (Section~\ref{sec:related}), and none of them reads clustering scores against a hand-crafted baseline. The contribution of this study is an evaluation protocol for label-free ECG structure, applied to these representations. We propose no new algorithm. The protocol has four parts:
+\begin{enumerate}
+\item Each chance-corrected clustering score is read against the cluster sizes and against the supervised ceiling defined in Section~\ref{sec:clust}, the accuracy a linear classifier reaches on the same records and representation. The ceiling indicates whether a low score reflects little label information or label information that the clustering does not recover.
+\item Purity, the internal silhouette index and the conditioner, the scaling or transform applied to each feature before clustering, are tested as sources of misleading results. Purity read without the majority-class proportion and the silhouette of a degenerate solution account for the original project's numbers.
+\item Hand-crafted features are compared with two named foundation models on PTB-XL. On a MIT-BIH beat-morphology task, which the closest benchmark omits, they are compared with learned and raw representations.
+\item Four of the representations are tested under two deployment stressors, heavy class imbalance and a simulated federation with skewed class proportions.
+\end{enumerate}
 
 \section{Related work}\label{sec:related}
 
-\paragraph{Unsupervised ECG and representations.} Hand-crafted features built from
-RR-interval irregularity and beat morphology encode the defining signatures of common
-arrhythmias and underpin decades of automatic analysis \cite{hrvstandards1996,dechazal2004}.
-Tensor and wavelet decompositions have been combined with spectral clustering for
-unsupervised 12-lead classification \cite{wavelettensorecg2018}. Reconstruction
-autoencoders optimise signal energy rather than class structure, which makes them
-strong compressors but weak organisers of class geometry. Contrastive self-supervised
-methods built on physiologically motivated augmentations and patient- or lead-level
-invariances, including CLOCS \cite{clocs2021}, 3KG \cite{threekg2021}, and contrastive
-predictive coding for 12-lead ECG \cite{mehari2022ssl}, established the pretraining
-recipes that later foundation models scaled up. Self-supervised and foundation models for
-ECG, such as HuBERT-ECG \cite{hubertecg2024} and ECGFounder \cite{ecgfounder2024}, learn
-general representations from millions of recordings and are now evaluated as frozen
-feature extractors. The wavelet-tensor and spectral-clustering pipeline of
-\cite{wavelettensorecg2018} addresses a related unsupervised goal with a different
-(tensor) representation; we focus on the four families above, which span the energy-only
-to definition-encoding spectrum, and leave a tensor arm to future work.
-
-\paragraph{Clustering evaluation.} Cluster purity is not chance-corrected: assigning
-every point to the majority class attains a purity equal to that class's prevalence.
-External, chance-corrected indices, the adjusted Rand index \cite{hubertarabie1985} and
-adjusted mutual information \cite{vinh2010}, are near zero for such trivial solutions
-and are the appropriate headline metrics. Internal indices such as the silhouette
-\cite{rousseeuw1987} measure compactness in the embedding being clustered and can be
-maximised by degenerate solutions, so they require cluster-size sanity checks.
-
-\paragraph{Closest benchmark.} The ECG foundation-model benchmark of
-\cite{lookingbeyond2026} reports cluster-separability (ARI) of foundation-model
-embeddings on PTB-XL and finds that some models cluster by clinical label while others
-cluster by dataset. It compares foundation models to one another and states that prior
-work omits a representation-wise evaluation; it does not add a hand-crafted baseline,
-and it does not include MIT-BIH. We fill both gaps and add a supervised ceiling so that
-a low clustering score can be attributed to geometry rather than to missing information.
+\paragraph{Unsupervised ECG and representations.} Hand-crafted features built from RR-interval irregularity and beat morphology encode the defining signatures of common arrhythmias and underpin decades of automatic analysis \cite{hrvstandards1996,dechazal2004}. He et al.~\cite{wavelettensorecg2018} combined wavelet tensor decomposition with spectral clustering for unsupervised 12-lead classification. A reconstruction autoencoder is trained to reproduce its input signal, so its training objective holds no class information. Contrastive self-supervised methods are built on physiologically motivated augmentations and patient- or lead-level invariances. They include CLOCS \cite{clocs2021}, 3KG \cite{threekg2021}, and contrastive predictive coding for 12-lead ECG \cite{mehari2022ssl}. Later foundation models reuse some of these objectives: ECG-FM adopts the multi-segment contrastive objective of CLOCS \cite{McKeen_2025}, and ECG-CPC is pretrained with contrastive predictive coding \cite{arxiv_2509_25095}. Foundation models for ECG, such as HuBERT-ECG \cite{hubertecg2024} and ECGFounder \cite{ecgfounder2024,arxiv_2410_04133}, were pretrained on 9.1 million and 10\,771\,552 ECGs, respectively, and are evaluated as frozen feature extractors. The tensor representation of He et al. addresses a related unsupervised goal, and we leave a tensor representation to future work. The five representations studied here range from raw samples, which keep only amplitude and energy, to features that measure the defining criteria of the classes.
+
+\paragraph{Clustering evaluation.} Cluster purity is not chance-corrected: assigning every point to the majority class attains a purity equal to that class's prevalence. External, chance-corrected indices, the adjusted Rand index \cite{hubertarabie1985} and adjusted mutual information \cite{vinh2010}, have expectation 0 under random labelling and equal 0 when all points form one cluster (Table~\ref{tab:metrics}), and they are the appropriate headline metrics. Internal indices such as the silhouette \cite{rousseeuw1987} measure compactness in the embedding being clustered and can be maximised by degenerate solutions, so they require cluster-size sanity checks.
+
+\paragraph{ECG foundation-model benchmarks.} Several recent studies benchmark ECG foundation models on supervised downstream tasks. BenchECG, by Lunelli et al.~\cite{Lunelli_2026}, evaluates public models on eight datasets with 1\,674\,704 recordings. Al-Masud et al.~\cite{arxiv_2509_25095} compare eight foundation models on 26 tasks from 12 datasets, fine-tuned and frozen. Xu et al.~\cite{Xu_2025} compare language, general time-series and ECG foundation models with time-series deep-learning models. Berger et al.~\cite{arxiv_2602_17531} compared five pretrained ECG encoders with a randomly initialised encoder under linear evaluation and found the random encoder consistently competitive with two of them, CLOCS and HeartLang. A review of open ECG foundation models names the absence of standardised evaluation frameworks as a challenge \cite{Han_2026}. None of BenchECG, Al-Masud et al. and Berger et al. evaluates clustering of the embeddings or includes a hand-crafted baseline.
+
+\paragraph{Closest benchmark.} Filice et al.~\cite{lookingbeyond2026} evaluated frozen ECG foundation models as feature extractors on four 12-lead datasets, PTB-XL among them. The models were ECG-FM, ECGFounder, HuBERT-ECG in three sizes and the ECG-JEPA of Weimann and Conrad. Beside classifier probes under class-balanced 15-fold cross-validation, they reported the ARI of Gaussian-mixture clusters of the embeddings. They found that ECGFounder and ECG-FM separated their embeddings more by clinical label than by dataset, while HuBERT-ECG and ECG-JEPA separated them more by dataset than by label. Their benchmark compares foundation models with one another. The present study evaluates HuBERT-ECG and ECGFounder in the same frozen-extractor setting on PTB-XL and reads each clustering score against the supervised ceiling of Section~\ref{sec:clust}.
 
 \section{Methods}\label{sec:methods}
 
 \subsection{Data and cohorts}
 
-\paragraph{PTB-XL.} We use PTB-XL \cite{ptbxl2020}, a public 12-lead database
-(21\,799 records, 18\,869 patients; 10\,s strips). Rhythm labels are taken from the
-SCP-ECG statements. The SR-versus-AF cohort uses all records carrying an AF statement
-(AFIB or AFLT) as positives and sinus-rhythm records without any AF statement as
-controls. PTB-XL encodes rhythm statements at a likelihood of zero meaning ``present'',
-so a presence-based rule is required; a confidence threshold would discard every SR
-record. We draw a balanced, patient-disjoint cohort of 2\,542 records (1\,282 SR,
-1\,260 AF), so the majority-class baseline is 0.504. Lead II is used for rhythm and
-morphology. All evaluation is patient-grouped: no patient appears in more than one fold
-or in both classes.
-
-\paragraph{MIT-BIH.} For generalization we use the MIT-BIH Arrhythmia Database
-\cite{mitbih2001,physionet2000}. Each annotated beat is segmented into a window of
-$[-300, +250]$\,ms around the R-peak across both leads. We form a balanced
-normal-versus-premature-ventricular (N-vs-V) task of 3\,000 beats (1\,500 per class)
-from 47 records, with the record as the grouping unit. This is a morphology task and a
-deliberate contrast to the PTB-XL rhythm task.
-
-\subsection{Representations}
-
-We compare four families on PTB-XL (Table~\ref{tab:main}):
+\paragraph{PTB-XL.} We used PTB-XL, a public database of 10\,s 12-lead records, whose data descriptor reports 21\,837 records from 18\,885 patients \cite{ptbxl2020}. Its waveforms are stored in the WaveForm DataBase (WFDB) format as 16-bit samples at a resolution of \SI{1}{\micro\volt} per least significant bit. They are released at 500\,Hz and in a version downsampled to 100\,Hz \cite{ptbxl2020}. The benchmark used the 100\,Hz version of release 1.0.3 \cite{ptbxl103}. Each record was read with the WFDB reader, which applies the conversion to physical units given in the record header. The signal was stored as a 32-bit floating-point array of 1000 samples by 12 leads. No other conversion preceded the representation steps of Section~\ref{sec:repr}.
+
+Rhythm labels were taken from the statements of the Standard Communication Protocol for computer-assisted electrocardiography (SCP-ECG). The positives were drawn from the records carrying an atrial fibrillation (AFIB) or atrial flutter (AFLT) statement, and the controls from the sinus-rhythm records without one. The positive class, called AF in this paper, therefore pools atrial fibrillation and atrial flutter. PTB-XL sets the likelihood of every rhythm statement to zero \cite{ptbxl2020}, so a presence-based rule is required, and a confidence threshold would discard every SR record. The records were taken in random order with one record per patient. The sinus-rhythm records came first, as many as there are patients with an AF record, followed by the AF records of patients not yet included. The resulting balanced, patient-disjoint cohort holds 2\,542 records (1\,282 SR, 1\,260 AF), so the majority-class proportion is 0.504. No patient appears in both classes, and the supervised ceiling uses patient-grouped folds.
+
+We study this binary task because one clinical criterion defines atrial fibrillation. Atrial fibrillation is recognised on the ECG by irregular RR intervals and the absence of discernible repeating P waves \cite{Van_Gelder_2024}. With one defining criterion, the supervised ceiling and the chance-corrected score of Section~\ref{sec:clust} can be read against a known class difference. The atrial flutter records of the AF class fall outside this criterion, a limit of the task that Section~\ref{sec:limitations} states.
+
+\paragraph{MIT-BIH.} For generalization we used the MIT-BIH Arrhythmia Database \cite{mitbih2001,physionet2000}, whose two-lead records are sampled at 360\,Hz. The records were read with the WFDB reader into the physical signal and stored at 32-bit precision, in the same way as PTB-XL. Non-beat annotations were excluded. Each annotated beat was segmented into a window of $[-300, +250]$\,ms around the R peak across both leads. We formed a balanced task of normal versus premature ventricular beats (N-versus-V). At most 400 beats per record and class were kept, and 1\,500 beats per class were then drawn at random, giving 3\,000 beats from 47 records. The record was the grouping unit. This task is defined by beat shape, and it is a deliberate contrast to the PTB-XL rhythm task.
+
+\subsection{Representations}\label{sec:repr}
+
+We compared five representations on PTB-XL, and the conditioner of Section~\ref{sec:clust} transformed each of them before clustering. Table~\ref{tab:repr} gives the input, dimension and pretraining of each. Both foundation models were used frozen, in evaluation mode, with no fine-tuning.
 \begin{itemize}
-\item \textbf{Raw flattening} reproduces the original pipeline: each lead is
-moving-average downsampled and all leads are concatenated into one vector
-($\sim$2000 dimensions). It carries amplitude and energy but no cross-recording
-temporal alignment.
-\item \textbf{Convolutional autoencoder} (conv-AE): a 1-D convolutional autoencoder
-with a 32-unit bottleneck, trained to convergence with a validation split. This is a
-properly specified reconstruction model, in contrast to the original collapsed dense
-autoencoder.
-\item \textbf{Hand-crafted} (16 features): RR-interval statistics and irregularity
-(SDNN, RMSSD, pNN50, coefficient of variation, RR entropy, mean heart rate), P-wave
-presence and amplitude, QRS width and amplitude, and beat-template consistency
-\cite{hrvstandards1996,dechazal2004}, extracted with NeuroKit2 \cite{neurokit2021}.
-\item \textbf{Foundation embeddings} (frozen): mean-pooled hidden states of HuBERT-ECG
-(768-d) \cite{hubertecg2024} and the 1024-d pooled features of ECGFounder
-\cite{ecgfounder2024}, both used as fixed feature extractors with no fine-tuning.
+\item \textbf{Raw flattening} reproduces the representation of the original project. Each lead was averaged over non-overlapping windows of six samples, which gives 166 values per lead from the 1000 samples, and the 12 leads were concatenated into one real-valued vector of 1992 values. It keeps amplitude and waveform energy at fixed sample positions, with no alignment of beats across records.
+\item \textbf{Convolutional autoencoder} (conv-AE). The 12-lead strip was resampled to 2500 samples per lead and z-scored per lead. Three strided one-dimensional convolution layers, global average pooling and a linear layer map it to a 32-value code. The network was trained to reconstruct its input under the mean squared error, so the code keeps what reconstruction needs. It takes the place of the dense autoencoder of the original project.
+\item \textbf{Hand-crafted features} (16 values) were computed from lead II. The signal was cleaned, R peaks were detected and the waves were delineated with the NeuroKit2 toolbox \cite{neurokit2021}, and we selected its discrete wavelet method for the delineation. The 16 features form two groups:
+\begin{itemize}
+\item Eight features describe the RR intervals \cite{hrvstandards1996}: the mean RR interval, the standard deviation of the RR intervals (SDNN), the root mean square of successive differences (RMSSD), the proportion of successive differences above 50\,ms (pNN50), the coefficient of variation of the RR intervals, the Shannon entropy of their histogram, the mean heart rate and the number of beats.
+\item Eight features describe the morphology \cite{dechazal2004}: the fraction of beats with a detected P wave, the mean and standard deviation of the P-wave amplitude, the mean QRS width, the mean and standard deviation of the R-peak amplitude, and the mean and standard deviation of the correlation of each beat with the median beat.
+\end{itemize}
+Together the 16 features encode the clinical criteria of rhythm regularity, P-wave presence and QRS shape. Missing values from a failed delineation were replaced by the column median.
+\item \textbf{HuBERT-ECG} \cite{hubertecg2024}, a self-supervised transformer modelled on the HuBERT model for speech representation learning, received the first 5\,s of the 12 leads at 100\,Hz. The leads were concatenated one after another into one sequence of 6000 samples, with no amplitude normalisation. The last hidden layer was averaged over time to 768 values.
+\item \textbf{ECGFounder} \cite{ecgfounder2024,arxiv_2410_04133}, a convolutional network pretrained with supervision on diagnostic labels, received the 10\,s 12-lead strip resampled from 100\,Hz to its 500\,Hz input (5000 samples per lead). The strip was z-scored with one mean and one standard deviation over all leads. The 1024 time-averaged features of the last convolutional stage were used, with the classification layer removed.
 \end{itemize}
-On MIT-BIH the rhythm features do not transfer to single beats, so we compare a raw
-beat window, a 21-dimensional beat-morphology and RR-context feature set, and the
-conv-AE. The 12-lead foundation models are not applied to 2-lead beats; this scoping
-limit is itself informative, because such models are tied to the 12-lead strip format.
-
-\subsection{Clustering and evaluation}
-
-Each representation is clustered with $k$-means, Ward agglomerative, spectral,
-Gaussian mixture, and HDBSCAN, with the number of clusters set to the number of classes
-\cite{sklearn2011}. The headline metrics are the chance-corrected adjusted Rand index
-(ARI) \cite{hubertarabie1985} and adjusted mutual information \cite{vinh2010};
-normalized mutual information, V-measure, Fowlkes-Mallows, purity, silhouette
-\cite{rousseeuw1987}, and the largest-cluster fraction are reported alongside. Every
-metric is computed against a majority and a random baseline. A supervised ceiling bounds how much of the
-label a probe recovers from the same features under the same patient-grouped five-fold
-protocol; we report both a linear probe (balanced logistic regression) and a non-linear
-probe (random forest), so that a high ceiling with a low ARI identifies a geometric
-rather than an informational failure. Each configuration is repeated over ten seeds, and
-the headline contrasts are tested with a paired Wilcoxon signed-rank test across seeds.
-Because heavy-tailed features can dominate Euclidean distance, we sweep four conditioners
-(Yeo-Johnson power, standard, quantile, robust) and report power scaling as the default
-unless stated otherwise. To check that fixing the cluster count to the number of classes
-does not flatter the comparison, we also report adjusted mutual information, which is
-comparable across cluster counts, for $K$ from 2 to 8. Because the natural SR-to-AF
-prevalence is imbalanced, we additionally evaluate on a 9:1 SR:AF subsample.
-
-\subsection{Federated robustness}
-
-To probe deployment under data siloing we use standard federated $k$-means with
-FedAvg-style centroid aggregation \cite{fedavg2017}; we do not propose a new federated
-method. The 2\,542 records are partitioned, patient-grouped, across ten simulated sites,
-either independently and identically distributed (IID) or with class proportions drawn
-from a Dirichlet distribution so that sites differ sharply in arrhythmia prevalence
-(non-IID). We report the global ARI relative to centralized clustering of the same
-representation.
+
+Only the hand-crafted features used a single lead. Raw flattening reproduces the 12-lead representation of the original project, and the conv-AE was given the same 12-lead strip. Both foundation models were pretrained on 12-lead input and were given all 12 leads. Their input still differed from their pretraining input: neither was filtered as in pretraining, the HuBERT-ECG input was not rescaled in amplitude, and the ECGFounder input was upsampled from 100\,Hz (Section~\ref{sec:limitations}). We computed the hand-crafted features from a single lead because the defining signs of atrial fibrillation can be read in one lead. The guideline accepts as diagnostic a single-lead tracing of at least 30\,s that shows them \cite{Van_Gelder_2024}. Atrial fibrillation and other rhythm classes have also been classified from single-lead recordings \cite{clifford2017af}. We chose lead II as that lead. Zheng et al.~\cite{Zheng_2020} likewise extracted features such as the atrial rate and the mean and variance of the RR interval from lead II. The single-lead set leaves out inter-lead information, such as the electrical axis and lead-specific morphology, which other diagnoses need. In exchange, it can be computed from a single-lead recording. In this benchmark the single-lead features were compared with representations that see all 12 leads. We did not test the effect of adding leads to the hand-crafted set.
+
+We chose the two foundation models by three criteria:
+\begin{enumerate}
+\item Both have public weights and take 12-lead input.
+\item They differ in pretraining and architecture: HuBERT-ECG is a self-supervised transformer and ECGFounder a supervised convolutional network.
+\item Both are evaluated in the closest benchmark \cite{lookingbeyond2026}, which reports ECGFounder's embedding as organised by clinical label and the embedding of HuBERT-ECG as organised by dataset.
+\end{enumerate}
+We included ECGFounder because of that report, so that the hand-crafted features are compared with a foundation model whose embedding follows the clinical label.
+
+On MIT-BIH the rhythm features do not apply to single beats, so we compared three beat representations. The raw beat window flattens both leads over the $[-300, +250]$\,ms window, 198 samples per lead at 360\,Hz, into one vector of 396 values. It keeps the amplitude at fixed positions around the R peak. Heartbeats of the MIT-BIH database have been classified from two-lead feature sets of morphology and RR intervals \cite{dechazal2004}. We defined 21 hand-crafted beat features in these two groups:
+\begin{itemize}
+\item Five RR-context values measure the prematurity of a beat: the previous and next RR intervals, their ratio, the ratio of the previous interval to the local mean of up to six preceding intervals, and that local mean.
+\item Eight morphology values per lead describe the shape of the beat: the R amplitude, the energy, the QRS width, the maximum, the minimum, the skewness and the kurtosis of the beat window, and the correlation of the window with the median of all 3\,000 beats of the task.
+\end{itemize}
+The conv-AE was applied to the beat window as on PTB-XL. The 550\,ms two-lead window was resampled to 2500 samples per lead and z-scored per lead before the same network, and the code again keeps what reconstruction needs. We did not apply the foundation models to the two-lead beats, because their input is a 12-lead strip.
