+\subsection{Clustering and evaluation}\label{sec:clust}
+
+Each representation was clustered with five clustering algorithms \cite{sklearn2011}: $k$-means, Ward agglomerative clustering (Ward), spectral clustering, a Gaussian mixture model (GMM) and hierarchical density-based spatial clustering of applications with noise (HDBSCAN) \cite{hdbscan2013}. For $k$-means, Ward, spectral clustering and the GMM the number of clusters was set to the number of classes. HDBSCAN is given no cluster count. It labels low-density points as noise, and we set its minimum cluster size to the larger of 10 and one fiftieth of the number of records. The noise points kept their own label and were never reassigned to a cluster. For the ARI, the adjusted mutual information (AMI), the normalized mutual information (NMI), the V-measure and the Fowlkes-Mallows index (FMI), they therefore form one additional cluster. A solution that labels most points as noise then scores near zero. Purity counts noise points as misassigned. The silhouette is computed on the non-noise points. The largest-cluster fraction counts only non-noise clusters, over all records. We tuned no setting of a clustering algorithm against the labels, and Table~\ref{tab:settings} gives the settings of every step. We chose the default conditioner on a labelled pilot analysis, described below.
+
+The headline metrics are the ARI \cite{hubertarabie1985} and the AMI \cite{vinh2010}, which are corrected for chance. Every metric is read against its value under random labelling and under a one-cluster solution, and Table~\ref{tab:metrics} gives these values with the meaning, formula and range of each metric. The NMI \cite{Danon_2005}, the V-measure \cite{rosenberg2007vmeasure}, the FMI \cite{Fowlkes_1983}, purity, the silhouette \cite{rousseeuw1987} and the largest-cluster fraction are reported alongside. With the arithmetic-mean normalisation used here, the NMI equals the V-measure, so the two coincide in every result. The ARI is compared with a random labelling, drawn uniformly over the clusters, whose mean ARI over ten seeds is 0.000. Purity is compared with the majority-class proportion, 0.504.
+
+A supervised ceiling measures how much of the label a linear classifier recovers from the same representation under the default conditioner. It is the accuracy of a balanced logistic regression under stratified, patient-grouped five-fold cross-validation, and its chance level is the majority-class proportion. A random forest on the same folds is reported as a non-linear check. The ceiling and the ARI are read together. A high ceiling with a low ARI suggests that the label is linearly decodable but does not lie along the directions of largest variance, which $k$-means and the GMM follow. We call this case a failure of geometry. A ceiling near its chance level suggests that little of the label is linearly decodable, although a non-linear classifier can still recover label information that the ceiling misses (Section~\ref{sec:sep}). Both readings hold for the default conditioner, since another conditioner can change the ARI of the same representation (Section~\ref{sec:cond}).
+
+In the main benchmark each fit of $k$-means, spectral clustering and the GMM was repeated over ten seeds. The cohort, the raw and hand-crafted features, the two foundation-model embeddings and the trained conv-AE were fixed across seeds. The seeds changed only the random initialisation of $k$-means, spectral clustering and the GMM. Ward and HDBSCAN are deterministic and were fitted once. The seed spread therefore measures the stability of the clustering step on a fixed cohort, and it does not measure the sampling uncertainty of the cohort. The headline contrasts were tested with a paired, two-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test on the per-seed $k$-means ARI. With ten paired seeds the smallest attainable p-value is 0.002. A p-value at this floor gives the direction of a contrast and says nothing about its size. The difference in mean ARI is therefore reported as the effect size of each contrast.
+
+Because heavy-tailed features can dominate Euclidean distance, we swept four conditioners: the Yeo-Johnson power transform, standard scaling, a quantile transform to a normal output and robust scaling. Each was fitted on the whole cohort. The power transform is the default unless stated otherwise. We chose it after a pilot analysis on a smaller, labelled SR-versus-AF set, in which robust scaling collapsed the $k$-means partition of the hand-crafted features. Because this choice used the labels, all four conditioners are reported in the Results.
+
+Two further analyses test settings of the main benchmark, each with one fit. We report the AMI of $k$-means for $K$ from 2 to 8, with one fit per $K$. Because the AMI is comparable across cluster counts, this checks that fixing the cluster count to the number of classes does not flatter the comparison. The natural SR-to-AF prevalence is imbalanced, so we also evaluated a 9:1 SR:AF subsample. It holds all SR records and one ninth as many AF records, drawn once at random. It was conditioned on its own records and clustered once with $k$-means. The $K$ sweep, the random-forest check and the imbalance analysis cover raw flattening, the hand-crafted features and the two foundation models.
