-\subsection{Robustness under deployment stress: imbalance and federation}
-
-Two stressors closer to deployment separate the representations more sharply than the
-balanced benchmark, and both favour the cheap features. First, class imbalance. At the
-natural-like 9:1 SR:AF ratio (majority 0.90), every representation's ARI falls, because
-isolating a 10\% minority cluster with a partitional method is intrinsically hard, but the
-hand-crafted features retain the most structure (ARI 0.232, AMI 0.214) and now
-\emph{overtake} ECGFounder (ARI 0.060, AMI 0.143), with HuBERT-ECG and raw near zero. The
-foundation embedding's balanced-data lead does not survive imbalance.
-
-Second, federation. Under IID federation across ten sites, every representation retains
-essentially its centralized ARI, which validates the federated procedure
-(Table~\ref{tab:fed}; Fig.~\ref{fig:fed}). Non-IID client heterogeneity degrades all
-representations, but unequally. The hand-crafted features are the most robust, retaining
-92\% of their centralized ARI (0.644 to 0.591). ECGFounder's centralized advantage erodes
-from 0.885 to 0.612, so that under realistic site heterogeneity the 1024-dimensional
-foundation embedding (0.612) and the 16 hand-crafted features (0.591) are nearly tied.
-Across both stressors the compact interpretable representation is the more dependable
-choice for privacy-preserving, multi-site phenotyping.
+\subsection{Class imbalance and simulated federation}
+
+Two stressors closer to deployment changed the comparison between ECGFounder and the hand-crafted features. In one imbalanced subsample the hand-crafted features exceeded ECGFounder, and under the simulated federation the two came close. The first stressor was class imbalance. At a 9:1 SR:AF ratio (majority-class proportion 0.90), every representation's ARI fell. The hand-crafted features retained the most structure (ARI 0.232, AMI 0.214) and exceeded ECGFounder (ARI 0.060, AMI 0.143), with HuBERT-ECG and raw flattening near zero. This analysis rests on one random subsample and one $k$-means fit, so it has no seed spread. It indicates, pending replication on further subsamples, that ECGFounder's lead on balanced data may not hold under this imbalance.
+
+The second stressor was the simulated federation (Table~\ref{tab:fed}, Fig.~\ref{fig:fed}). Under the IID split across ten sites, every representation kept its centralized ARI to two decimals, which checks the federated procedure. The non-IID class-proportion skew lowered every non-zero result, but unequally. The hand-crafted features kept 0.92 of their centralized ARI (0.644 to 0.591, seed standard deviation 0.067). ECGFounder fell from 0.885 to 0.612 with a seed standard deviation of 0.350, so its federated result varied strongly across partitions and initialisations. Under this skew ECGFounder (0.612) and the hand-crafted features (0.591) came close, and the hand-crafted features varied less across seeds (0.067 against 0.350), within this simulation on one dataset.
 
